{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791001031,"nonce":"K0o+HypjtRgUTerweI069ShmZIk=","title":"A leaf called once from the interpreter is never promoted once compiled callers own it","message":"`tiered_compile_tier_inner` skips the full LLVM promote after publishing the re-tier entry when `calls \u003c= 1` (1c52a4f, git-bug e510c5f), to spare `main` -- a loop owner entered once -- a promote nothing would use. `calls` counts interpreted entries only. A leaf with its own hot loop that the interpreter entered once before its callers compiled (seen with `shape=caller-loop`) matches the same test; its later calls all come from compiled callers, which tick nothing, so the promote is never asked for again and the function runs on Cranelift for the rest of the run.\n\n**Reproducer:** PoolBench.hx (source on 8d19050), `ash --mode hybrid poolbench.hl 4 2000 20000` with `ASH_TIER_LOG=1`: findex 31 (`job`) logs `demand shape=caller-loop`, then `defer findex=31 tier=llvm reason=handed-off`, and never installs at LLVM. With work=2000 the same function is promoted at ~45 ms (it is entered more than once before the hand-off).\n\nMac, serial time per round of 4 calls at work=20000: hybrid 280 us against AOT 75 us. NUC: hybrid 148-219 us against AOT 111 us.","files":null}]}